Description: Fix CVE-2020-36851 — block SSRF to internal/reserved/metadata targets
 cors-anywhere 0.4.4 forwards unauthenticated client-specified requests to any
 target host with no default deny-list, allowing SSRF against RFC1918/link-local
 ranges and cloud-metadata (IMDS) endpoints (CWE-918). There is no official
 upstream fix (maintainer treats this as a deployment concern); this novel patch
 implements the mitigation recommended by GHSA-9wmg-93pw-fc3g /
 GHSA-r3jv-xfgx-gj24: a secure-by-default destination filter
 (isForbiddenTarget) that rejects private/link-local/CGNAT/reserved IPv4 & IPv6
 literals (incl. IPv4-mapped dotted and hex forms), and cloud-metadata
 hostnames/endpoints called out in the advisory (169.254.169.254,
 fd00:ec2::254, metadata.google.internal, 192.0.0.192, 100.100.100.200).
 Enforced in the request handler before proxying and when internally following
 redirects; operators may opt out via blockPrivateTargets:false. Loopback
 (127.0.0.0/8, ::1) remains allowed for legitimate local-development proxying.
Origin: ai-synthesized, CVE-2020-36851
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-07-30

